findings.evidence:
The key is the full remote address, not the client identity and not even the client's address:
  20: if !allow(r.RemoteAddr, config.RateLimitBurst) {
http.Request.RemoteAddr is host:port, and the port is a fresh ephemeral one per TCP connection, so every reconnection produces a key the map has never seen and allow() at middleware/limits.go:29-32 seeds it with a full burst again. In the other direction, nothing ever gives a bucket back: allow() only ever decrements (line 36), there is no timer, no ticker, no timestamp on the entry and no eviction anywhere in the file, so once a key reaches zero, line 33 denies it forever for the life of the process.
